BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L3 — Family member counting rule (six generations = one shareholder)
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Hawthorne Corp has 85 individual shareholders on record, including a family group of 12 members spanning four generations. Under IRC Section 1361(c)(1), how many shareholders does Hawthorne count for the 100-shareholder limit?', '["74, because the entire family group counts as one shareholder", "85, because each individual is counted separately", "73, because the family counts as zero additional shareholders", "81, because only spouses count as one shareholder"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1361(c)(1), members of a family spanning up to six generations are treated as a single shareholder for the 100-shareholder limit. The 12 family members count as one, so Hawthorne has 85 − 12 + 1 = 74 shareholders. Choice B is incorrect because the family aggregation rule was enacted specifically to allow closely held family businesses to use S corporations without exceeding the shareholder cap.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — ESBT eligibility with beneficiary counting
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Ridgemont Corp is an S corporation with 95 individual shareholders. Shareholder Franklin transfers her shares into an electing small business trust (ESBT) with five potential current beneficiaries. Under IRC Section 1361(c)(2), what is the shareholder count after the transfer?', '["The S election terminates because trusts cannot hold S corporation stock", "The transfer requires IRS approval before taking effect", "99, because each ESBT beneficiary is counted as a separate shareholder", "95, because the ESBT counts as a single shareholder"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 1361(c)(2)(B)(v), an ESBT is an eligible S corporation shareholder, but each potential current beneficiary is counted as a separate shareholder for the 100-shareholder limit. Removing Franklin (1) and adding five beneficiaries yields 95 − 1 + 5 = 99 shareholders. Choice D incorrectly treats the ESBT as a single shareholder, which applies to qualified Subchapter S trusts (QSSTs), not ESBTs.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Nondeductible expenses reducing basis
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Stratton Inc., an S corporation, reports ordinary income of $60,000, a $4,000 penalty for late tax filing, and a $6,000 distribution for 2026. Shareholder Monroe, the sole owner with beginning basis of $25,000, asks how the penalty affects stock basis. What is Monroe''s ending stock basis?', '["$85,000", "$79,000", "$75,000", "$81,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 1367, shareholder basis is increased by income items then decreased by distributions, nondeductible/noncapital expenses, and deductible losses. The $4,000 penalty is a nondeductible expense that reduces basis without providing a tax deduction. Basis: $25,000 + $60,000 − $6,000 − $4,000 = $75,000. Choice B ($79,000) incorrectly omits the penalty reduction, treating nondeductible expenses as having no basis effect.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Form 1120-S informational return
(3, 'S Corporations', 'What form does an S corporation file as its federal income tax return?', '["Form 1120", "Form 1120-S", "Form 1065", "Schedule C"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Subchapter S of the IRC, an S corporation files Form 1120-S as an informational return. The corporation itself generally pays no federal income tax; instead, income and deductions pass through to shareholders via Schedule K-1. Form 1120 (choice A) is filed by C corporations, Form 1065 by partnerships, and Schedule C by sole proprietors.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — C corp NOL carryforwards suspended during S years
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Granite Industries operated as a C corporation for 10 years, accumulating a $120,000 net operating loss carryforward. Granite elects S corporation status on January 1, 2026. In 2026, the S corporation earns $200,000 of ordinary income. Evaluate whether Granite can use the C corporation NOL to offset the 2026 S corporation income and explain the policy rationale.', '["The NOL cannot offset S corporation pass-through income but is preserved for use if Granite reverts to C status", "The NOL offsets $120,000 of the pass-through income, reducing each shareholder''s K-1 income", "The NOL is permanently lost upon the S election because the entity changes tax character", "The NOL offsets the S corporation''s built-in gains tax but not the pass-through income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1371(b)(1), C corporation attributes such as NOL carryforwards are suspended during S corporation years and cannot offset S corporation pass-through income. The NOL is preserved and available if the corporation reverts to C status. This prevents a double benefit where shareholders would deduct losses generated at the corporate level. Choice B incorrectly allows the C corp NOL to flow through to shareholders.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Elect to distribute E&P before AAA (bypass election)
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Beacon Corp, an S corporation with accumulated C corporation E&P of $80,000, has an AAA of $50,000. The corporation wants to eliminate its accumulated E&P to avoid the excess net passive income tax under Section 1375. Beacon''s sole shareholder has stock basis of $200,000. Evaluate the available election and its tax consequences if Beacon distributes $80,000.', '["Beacon must follow the standard ordering rules and cannot choose to bypass the AAA", "Beacon can elect to distribute from E&P first, but only the amount exceeding AAA ($30,000) is treated as a dividend", "Beacon should make a Section 338 election to eliminate the accumulated E&P without dividend treatment", "Beacon can elect under Section 1368(e)(3) to distribute from E&P first, making $80,000 taxable as a dividend and eliminating the E&P"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1368(e)(3), S corporation shareholders can elect to distribute from accumulated E&P before the AAA, making the entire $80,000 distribution a taxable dividend. This strategic election eliminates accumulated E&P, removing the risk of the Section 1375 excess net passive income tax and the three-year automatic termination rule. Choice A is incorrect because the Code specifically provides this bypass election to help S corporations purge old C corporation E&P.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Section 179 as separately stated item
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Oakridge Construction Inc., an S corporation, purchases $150,000 of qualifying equipment in 2026 and elects the Section 179 deduction. Shareholder Walsh owns 40% of the stock. How is the Section 179 deduction reported to Walsh?', '["It is included in ordinary business income on the K-1", "It is separately stated on Walsh''s Schedule K-1 as a Section 179 deduction of $60,000", "It reduces the corporation''s AAA but does not appear on the K-1", "It is limited to Walsh''s stock basis and reported as a capital loss"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 1366, the Section 179 deduction is a separately stated item on Schedule K-1 because each shareholder must apply their own Section 179 limitations at the individual level. Walsh receives 40% × $150,000 = $60,000 on her K-1. Choice A is incorrect because separately stated items are not netted into ordinary business income — they pass through individually so shareholders can apply personal limitations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Distributions cannot reduce debt basis
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Shepherd Holdings Inc., an S corporation, distributes $45,000 to its sole shareholder Palmer in 2026. Palmer has stock basis of $30,000 and debt basis of $20,000 from a direct loan to the corporation. How is the $45,000 distribution treated?', '["$30,000 is tax-free return of basis, $15,000 reduces debt basis to $5,000", "$30,000 is tax-free return of basis, $15,000 is treated as capital gain", "$45,000 is tax-free because combined stock and debt basis exceeds the distribution", "$30,000 is tax-free, $15,000 is a taxable dividend"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 1368, distributions reduce stock basis but cannot reduce debt basis. Palmer''s stock basis of $30,000 absorbs $30,000 tax-free, reducing stock basis to zero. The remaining $15,000 exceeds stock basis and is treated as capital gain from the sale of stock. Choice A is incorrect because distributions never reduce debt basis — only pass-through losses may be deducted against debt basis under Section 1367(b)(2).', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Late-filed S election effective date
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Crestview Corp, a calendar-year C corporation, files Form 2553 on April 20, 2026, to elect S corporation status. All shareholders consented. When does the S election become effective?', '["January 1, 2026", "April 20, 2026", "January 1, 2027", "The election is invalid and must be refiled"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 1362(b), an S election must be filed by the 15th day of the third month of the tax year (March 15 for calendar-year corporations) to be effective for the current year. Since Crestview filed on April 20, after the March 15 deadline, the election is effective for the following tax year beginning January 1, 2027. Choice A is incorrect because the late filing disqualifies current-year effectiveness.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — At-risk limitation (Section 465) as second tier
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Mercer Corp, an S corporation, passes through a $90,000 loss to sole shareholder Dixon. Dixon has stock basis of $100,000 but is at-risk for only $70,000 because $30,000 of Dixon''s investment was financed with a nonrecourse loan from a related party. How much loss can Dixon deduct currently?', '["$90,000", "$70,000", "$100,000", "$60,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the four-tier S corporation loss limitation, losses first must not exceed stock and debt basis (Section 1366(d)), then must not exceed the at-risk amount (Section 465). Dixon''s stock basis of $100,000 passes the first test, but the at-risk amount is only $70,000 because the $30,000 nonrecourse related-party loan is excluded from at-risk. The $20,000 excess is suspended. Choice A ignores the at-risk limitation entirely.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Five-year re-election waiting period after termination
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Northfield Corp lost its S election on June 15, 2024, when a second class of stock was inadvertently created. The corporation corrected the issue immediately and applied for inadvertent termination relief under Section 1362(f), but the IRS denied the request. Evaluate when Northfield is eligible to re-elect S status without IRS consent and what options exist before that date.', '["Northfield may re-elect immediately because the termination was corrected", "Northfield is permanently barred from re-electing S status after an involuntary termination", "Northfield may re-elect after three years, beginning January 1, 2028", "Northfield may re-elect for the tax year beginning January 1, 2030, or earlier with IRS consent under Section 1362(g)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1362(g), a corporation whose S election terminates cannot re-elect S status for five tax years without IRS consent. Since the termination occurred during 2024, the first eligible year without consent is the tax year beginning January 1, 2030. The IRS may grant earlier re-election for good cause. Choice C incorrectly states a three-year period, which is the threshold for passive income automatic termination, not the re-election bar.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Voting vs. nonvoting stock permitted
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Sterling Advisors Inc., an S corporation, wants to issue nonvoting common stock with identical economic rights to its existing voting common stock. Under IRC Section 1361(c)(4), what is the effect on Sterling''s S election?', '["The S election is unaffected because differences solely in voting rights do not create a second class of stock", "The S election terminates because two types of stock are outstanding", "The S election continues only if the nonvoting shares are held by family members", "The S election terminates unless the IRS grants prior approval"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1361(c)(4), differences in voting rights among shares of common stock are disregarded when determining whether an S corporation has more than one class of stock. As long as the shares confer identical rights to distributions and liquidation proceeds, voting and nonvoting common stock are treated as a single class. Choice B is incorrect because the one-class-of-stock rule focuses on economic rights, not governance rights.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Passive activity loss limitation at shareholder level
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Preston holds a 30% interest in Crescent Services Inc., an S corporation. Preston does not materially participate in Crescent''s operations. Crescent passes through a $50,000 ordinary loss to Preston. Preston has $60,000 stock basis and $10,000 of passive income from another source. How much of the loss can Preston deduct?', '["$50,000", "$0", "$10,000", "$60,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469, S corporation losses from activities in which the shareholder does not materially participate are passive losses, deductible only against passive income. Although Preston''s $60,000 basis exceeds the $50,000 loss (passing the basis limitation), the passive activity rules restrict the current deduction to $10,000 of available passive income. The remaining $40,000 is suspended. Choice A ignores the passive activity limitation, which applies at the shareholder level.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Ineligible corporations (DISC)
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Which type of corporation is ineligible to elect S corporation status under IRC Section 1361(b)(2)?', '["A professional services corporation", "A technology startup corporation", "A domestic international sales corporation (DISC)", "A corporation with a single shareholder"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 1361(b)(2), certain financial institutions, insurance companies, and domestic international sales corporations (DISCs) are ineligible to elect S corporation status. A DISC is specifically excluded because it already receives preferential tax treatment for export activities. Choices A and B describe entities that are not barred from S election as long as they meet all other eligibility requirements.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — All shareholders must consent to S election
(3, 'S Corporations', 'What is required for a corporation to make a valid S corporation election?', '["Consent of shareholders holding more than 50% of shares", "Board of directors approval only", "Consent of all shareholders on the date of election", "Approval from the IRS before filing Form 2553"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 1362(a)(2), an S election requires the consent of all shareholders who hold stock on the date the election is made. Each shareholder must sign Form 2553. Choice A describes the requirement for revoking an S election (more than 50%), not the initial election. Unanimous consent ensures no shareholder is involuntarily subjected to pass-through taxation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — S corp income not subject to SE tax
(3, 'S Corporations', 'How is an S corporation shareholder''s distributive share of ordinary business income treated for self-employment tax purposes?', '["It is not subject to self-employment tax", "It is subject to self-employment tax at the full 15.3% rate", "It is subject to self-employment tax only if the shareholder materially participates", "It is exempt from all payroll taxes including FICA on wages"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1402(a), an S corporation shareholder''s distributive share of ordinary business income is not subject to self-employment tax. This distinguishes S corporations from partnerships, where general partners pay self-employment tax on their distributive share. Shareholder-employees must still receive reasonable compensation as wages subject to FICA. Choice C incorrectly applies partnership self-employment rules to S corporations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — OAA tracks tax-exempt income separately from AAA
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Evergreen Corp, an S corporation with accumulated C corporation E&P, earns $40,000 of ordinary income and $10,000 of tax-exempt municipal bond interest in 2026. The beginning AAA is $20,000 and OAA is $5,000. What are the ending AAA and OAA balances before any distributions?', '["AAA increases to $70,000; OAA remains at $5,000", "AAA increases to $60,000; OAA remains at $5,000", "AAA increases to $70,000; OAA increases to $15,000", "AAA increases to $60,000; OAA increases to $15,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1368(e), AAA is increased by taxable income items but not by tax-exempt income. The $40,000 ordinary income increases AAA from $20,000 to $60,000. The $10,000 tax-exempt interest increases OAA from $5,000 to $15,000. Tax-exempt income is tracked in the OAA to preserve its tax-free character upon distribution. Choice A incorrectly adds both taxable and tax-exempt income to the AAA.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Excess business loss limitation (Section 461(l))
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Whitaker, a single filer, is the sole shareholder of Cascade Corp, an S corporation. Cascade passes through an ordinary business loss of $500,000 for 2026. Whitaker has stock basis of $600,000, is fully at risk, materially participates, and has no other business income. Evaluate the loss deduction after applying all four tiers of S corporation loss limitations, including the Section 461(l) excess business loss threshold of $305,000.', '["$305,000 currently deductible; $195,000 converted to an NOL carryforward", "$500,000 fully deductible because basis and at-risk amounts are sufficient", "$305,000 currently deductible; $195,000 permanently disallowed", "$600,000 deductible up to the full stock basis amount"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The $500,000 loss passes the basis test ($600,000), at-risk test (fully at risk), and passive activity test (materially participates). However, Section 461(l) limits net business losses for single filers to $305,000. The $195,000 excess converts to an NOL carryforward under Section 172, not a permanent disallowance. Choice B ignores the fourth-tier excess business loss limitation, which applies even when basis and at-risk amounts are sufficient.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: easy / L2 — Estate as eligible S corp shareholder
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Harper, a shareholder in Redwood Inc., an S corporation, passes away in 2026. Harper''s estate inherits the S corporation shares. Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1), is the estate an eligible S corporation shareholder?', '["No, only living individuals may hold S corporation stock", "Yes, but only for a two-year transition period", "No, the shares must be immediately transferred to an individual", "Yes, estates are eligible S corporation shareholders"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(B), an estate is an eligible S corporation shareholder. There is no statutory time limit on how long an estate may hold S corporation stock, though estates typically distribute assets within a reasonable administration period. Choice A is incorrect because the statute explicitly includes estates alongside individuals, certain trusts, and tax-exempt organizations as permissible shareholders.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Property contribution basis (Section 351/358)
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Thornton contributes equipment with a fair market value of $80,000 and an adjusted basis of $45,000 to newly formed Pinecrest Corp, an S corporation, in exchange for all the stock in a tax-free Section 351 exchange. What is Thornton''s initial stock basis in Pinecrest?', '["$80,000", "$45,000", "$62,500", "$0"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 358, when property is contributed to a corporation in a tax-free Section 351 exchange, the shareholder''s basis in the stock received equals the adjusted basis of the property transferred. Thornton''s stock basis is $45,000, the carryover basis of the contributed equipment. Choice A incorrectly uses fair market value, which would apply only in a taxable exchange. The carryover basis preserves the built-in gain for future recognition.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Suspended losses carry forward indefinitely
(3, 'S Corporations', 'In 2025, shareholder Quinn''s share of loss from Ridgeway Corp, an S corporation, was $40,000. Quinn''s stock and debt basis totaled $25,000, so $15,000 was suspended. In 2026, Ridgeway passes through $10,000 of ordinary income to Quinn. What happens to the suspended loss?', '["The entire $15,000 suspended loss becomes deductible", "$10,000 of the suspended loss becomes deductible; $5,000 remains suspended", "The suspended loss expired at the end of 2025", "The suspended loss is permanently disallowed because it exceeded basis in the prior year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 1366(d)(2), S corporation losses suspended due to insufficient basis carry forward indefinitely and become deductible as basis is restored. Quinn''s 2026 income of $10,000 increases stock basis, allowing $10,000 of the prior suspended loss to be deducted. The remaining $5,000 carries forward. Choice C is incorrect because there is no expiration period for basis-limited suspended losses — they carry forward until basis is restored or stock is disposed of.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — NUBIG cap on cumulative BIG tax
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Atlas Corp, a former C corporation, elected S status on January 1, 2024, with net unrealized built-in gain (NUBIG) of $300,000. In Year 2, Atlas recognized $200,000 of built-in gain from asset sales. In Year 3, Atlas sells another built-in gain asset for a gain of $180,000. Evaluate whether the full $180,000 is subject to the Section 1374 built-in gains tax.', '["Only $100,000 is subject to the BIG tax because cumulative net recognized built-in gain is capped at the $300,000 NUBIG", "The full $180,000 is subject to the BIG tax because each year''s gain is independently assessed", "No BIG tax applies because the five-year recognition period has a two-year lookback exception", "$150,000 is subject to the BIG tax based on a pro-rata annual allocation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1374(c)(2), the net recognized built-in gain for any tax year cannot exceed the NUBIG reduced by gains recognized in prior years. Atlas had $300,000 NUBIG and recognized $200,000 in Year 2, leaving $100,000 remaining. In Year 3, only $100,000 of the $180,000 gain is subject to the 21% BIG tax. The $80,000 excess is post-conversion gain taxed only at the shareholder level. Choice B ignores the cumulative NUBIG cap.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — More-than-2% shareholder-employee fringe benefits
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Magnolia Corp, an S corporation, pays $8,000 in group-term life insurance premiums for Dawson, a shareholder-employee who owns 65% of the stock. How are the premiums treated for tax purposes?', '["Excluded from Dawson''s income as an employee fringe benefit", "Deductible by the corporation but not reported on Dawson''s W-2", "Treated as a distribution reducing Dawson''s stock basis", "Included in Dawson''s W-2 wages as taxable compensation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1372, an S corporation shareholder owning more than 2% of the stock is treated like a partner for fringe benefit purposes. Fringe benefits such as group-term life insurance must be included in the shareholder-employee''s W-2 as taxable wages. Choice A is incorrect because the employee fringe benefit exclusion under Section 79 does not apply to more-than-2% S corporation shareholders, who are treated as self-employed for these purposes.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: hard / L3 — Mid-year termination pro-rata allocation
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Falcon Corp was an S corporation until its S election terminated on September 30, 2026, when an ineligible shareholder acquired stock. Falcon earned $365,000 of ordinary income for the full year ($300,000 through September 30 and $65,000 from October 1 to December 31). Absent a closing-of-the-books election, how is the income allocated between the S short year and C short year?', '["$300,000 to the S short year and $65,000 to the C short year", "$273,000 to the S short year and $92,000 to the C short year", "$182,500 to each short year", "The full $365,000 is reported on the S corporation return"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 1362(e)(2), when an S election terminates mid-year, the default allocation is the pro-rata (per-diem) method. Income is allocated based on the number of days in each short year: S year = 273/365 × $365,000 = $273,000; C year = 92/365 × $365,000 = $92,000. Choice A incorrectly uses actual income earned in each period, which is permitted only if the corporation and all affected shareholders elect the closing-of-the-books method.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — AAA cannot go negative from distributions (but can from losses)
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Sequoia Corp, an S corporation with accumulated E&P of $40,000, begins 2026 with AAA of $30,000. During 2026, Sequoia reports $20,000 of ordinary income and makes a $60,000 cash distribution. What is the ending AAA balance and how much of the distribution is treated as a taxable dividend?', '["AAA is $0; $10,000 is a taxable dividend", "AAA is ($10,000); $0 is a taxable dividend", "AAA is $0; $40,000 is a taxable dividend", "AAA is $50,000; no dividend treatment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1368(e)(1), AAA is first increased by income: $30,000 + $20,000 = $50,000. The $60,000 distribution reduces AAA to zero — distributions cannot make AAA negative. The first $50,000 from AAA is tax-free, and the remaining $10,000 is distributed from accumulated E&P and taxed as a dividend under Section 301. Choice B is incorrect because AAA cannot be reduced below zero by distributions; only losses can create a negative AAA.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Grantor trust as eligible S corp shareholder
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Which type of trust is an eligible S corporation shareholder under IRC Section 1361(c)(2)?', '["A complex trust with multiple corporate beneficiaries", "A foreign trust established by a nonresident alien", "A charitable remainder trust", "A grantor trust"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1361(c)(2)(A)(i), a grantor trust is an eligible S corporation shareholder because the trust is treated as owned by the grantor for income tax purposes, and the grantor (an individual) is counted as the shareholder. Choice A is ineligible because corporate beneficiaries cannot be S corporation shareholders. Choice B fails the nonresident alien prohibition under Section 1361(b)(1)(C).', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Tax-exempt organization UBIT on S corp income
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Community Health Foundation, a Section 501(c)(3) tax-exempt organization, acquires 15% of the stock in Meridian Corp, an S corporation. Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(B), what is the effect on Meridian''s S election?', '["The S election terminates because tax-exempt organizations are ineligible shareholders", "The S election continues, and the foundation''s share of income is completely tax-exempt", "The S election continues, and the foundation pays unrelated business income tax on its share of S corporation income", "The S election is suspended until the foundation disposes of the stock"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(B), certain tax-exempt organizations described in Section 501(c)(3) are eligible S corporation shareholders. The S election is not terminated. However, under Section 512(e), the organization''s share of S corporation income is treated as unrelated business taxable income (UBIT), subject to tax. Choice A is incorrect because the 1996 amendments specifically added qualifying tax-exempt organizations as eligible shareholders.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Form 2553 election form
(3, 'S Corporations', 'What IRS form is used to elect S corporation status?', '["Form 1120-S", "Form 2553", "Form 8832", "Form 1065"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 1362(a), a corporation elects S status by filing Form 2553 with the consent of all shareholders. Form 1120-S (choice A) is the annual informational return filed by existing S corporations, not the election form. Form 8832 is used for entity classification elections (check-the-box), and Form 1065 is the partnership return.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Differences in voting rights permitted under single class of stock
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Under IRC Section 1361(c)(4), what type of difference among shares is permitted without violating the one-class-of-stock requirement?', '["Differences in distribution rights", "Differences in liquidation preferences", "Differences in dividend payment timing", "Differences in voting rights"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1361(c)(4), differences in voting rights among shares of common stock do not create a second class of stock. An S corporation may have voting and nonvoting shares as long as all shares confer identical rights to distributions and liquidation proceeds. Choices A and B would create a prohibited second class of stock because they affect economic rights, which is what the single-class rule protects.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Pass-through character preservation
(3, 'S Corporations', 'What is a key characteristic of pass-through taxation for S corporations?', '["Income and losses pass through to shareholders and retain their tax character", "The corporation pays tax at a flat 21% rate before distributing to shareholders", "Shareholders report income only when distributions are received", "All income is taxed as ordinary income regardless of its character at the entity level"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1366, S corporation income, deductions, gains, losses, and credits pass through to shareholders on Schedule K-1, retaining their original character. For example, capital gains remain capital gains on the shareholder''s return. Choice B describes C corporation taxation. Choice D is incorrect because separately stated items such as capital gains and charitable contributions maintain their character when reported on individual returns.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
